    {
      "id": "verbosity_explain_water",
      "category": "verbosity_fingerprint",
      "check_type": "length_range",
      "passed": false,
      "errored": false,
      "error": null,
      "latency_ms": 9268.5,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 638,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical compound made up of two elements: **hydrogen and oxygen**. Its chemical formula is **$\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$**, meaning every single molecule of water consists of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom.\n\nHowever, to humans, Earth, and all known life, water is much more than just a chemical formula. Here is a breakdown of what water is, why it matters, and how it behaves.\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Physical States of Water\nWater is famous for being the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three distinct states of matter within Earth's normal temperature range:\n*   **Liquid:** The fluid we drink, swim in, and use for rain (found in oceans, rivers, and lakes).\n*   **Solid (Ice):** Frozen water (found in glaciers, snow, ice cubes, and frost).\n*   **Gas (Vapor/Steam):** Water in the form of an invisible gas in the atmosphere or steam from boiling water.\n\n### 2. Unique and Amazing Properties\nWater has several unusual scientific properties that make it essential for life:\n*   **The \"Universal Solvent\":** Water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. Because of this, it acts as a transport system, carrying chemicals, minerals, and nutrients wherever it goes (in our bloodstreams, through plant roots, etc.).\n*   **Cohesion and Adhesion:** Water molecules like to stick to each other (cohesion—which creates surface tension, allowing some bugs to walk on water) and to other surfaces (adhesion—which helps water move up the roots of tall trees).\n*   **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat before its temperature changes significantly. This is why oceans help regulate the temperature of the entire planet, and why our bodies (which are mostly water) don’t overheat easily.\n*   **Expanding When Frozen:** Unlike almost every other substance, water expands and becomes *less dense* when it freezes. This is why ice floats. If ice sank, lakes and oceans would freeze from the bottom up, destroying all aquatic life.\n\n### 3. Water and Life\n*   **In Biology:** Life as we know it cannot exist without water. The human body is made up of roughly **60% water**. It cushions our brains, lubricates joints, regulates body temperature, and flushes out waste.\n*   **On Earth:** Water covers about **71% of the Earth's surface**, mostly in oceans and seas (which hold about 96.5% of all Earth's water). Only about 2.5% of Earth's water is freshwater, and most of that is locked away in glaciers or deep underground, leaving less than 1% easily accessible for human use.\n\n### Summary\nIn short, water is the lifeblood of our planet. It shapes our geography, drives our weather patterns, makes up the majority of our bodies, and is the fundamental requirement for all known living organisms."
